PicoNIX ON-DEVICE MANUAL - AGENT-FIRST INDEX

ROM reference for agents and humans; not Linux, ISO C or CPython.

START HERE
  agent          Tool selection, safe reasoning, error handling
  terminal-operations  Shared PTY, keys, state, recovery
  workflows      Task recipes: compile, memory, trace, devices
  programming    Write -> compile/interpret -> execute -> check
  limits         Memory, process, file, interpreter limits
  compatibility  Non-Linux command/language differences
  shell-syntax   Exact interactive vs MCP shell operators
  process-resources  Reserved vs used, slots vs RAM files
  gpio-board     Pico vs Pico W safe pin access

LANGUAGE SPECIFICATIONS (read before writing code)
  languages/c       Actual cc C subset, syntax, errors
  languages/asm     Actual as Thumb-1 subset and directives
  languages/python  Actual Python subset and built-ins

LANGUAGE EXAMPLES
  c-examples, asm-examples, python-examples  Program examples

PROGRAM EXAMPLES (source, commands, expected output)
  example-c-hello          Minimal C stdout + cc/as pipeline
  example-c-loops          C while/if/else and exact output
  example-c-functions      C pointer parameter and local update
  example-c-arrays         Char array indexing and output
  example-asm-hello        Thumb-1 SVC write and PC-relative data
  example-asm-stack        Thumb-1 push/pop and exit
  example-asm-pid          PID decimal printing
  example-python-basics    Python subset arithmetic/loops/conditions
  example-python-arrays    Fixed lists and bytes
  example-python-files     Bounded read of /proc
  example-python-temperature  Read /dev/adc/temp
  example-python-udp       Authenticated-intent loopback UDP demo
  example-agent-verify     LLM build, run and verify

OPERATING AND DEBUGGING
  memory          Safe bounded inspection, symbols, slots
  trace           Trace modes, display and failure diagnosis
  devices         Device ownership, GPIO, PWM, ADC, I2C
  network         TCP/UDP, Wi-Fi, loopback, diagnostics
  syscalls        System-call/API overview and FD handling
  troubleshooting Command, MCP and runtime failures
  vi             vi keys and literal substitution
  fd             fd navigation and viewer keys
  reversi        Reversi controls, PTY operation, turns and passes
  tictactoe      3x3 game vs COM
  maze           Maze and wall hits
  top            Live CPU and process monitor
  led-patterns   /dev/fbchar glyphs
  pty            Pseudoterminal interfaces

EXTENDED TASK-ORIENTED REFERENCES
  quickstart          First read-only probes + tool sequence
  command-reference   CLI classification, safe finite commands
  fs-workflow         Filesystem, redirects and bounded inspection
  process-workflow    Processes, slots and FDs
  memory-map          RAM map, symbols and safe dumps
  trace-workflow      Syscall tracing and interpretation
  c-debug             Compile/assemble/debug tiny C programs
  asm-debug           Debug Thumb-1 assembler and PIC issues
  python-debug        Interpreter limitations and diagnostics
  syscalls-files      FD, open/read/write/lseek/ioctl
  syscalls-process    Process, timing and terminal APIs
  syscalls-net        Socket and select semantics
  gpio-workflow       Pin ownership and GPIO safety
  pwm-workflow        GP18 buzzer / GP19 servo, shared PWM safety
  i2c-workflow        I2C scan, read/write/xfer limits
  adc-workflow        Temperature and ADC handling
  framebuffer-workflow LED matrix and scroll
  network-workflow    Network, listeners and diagnostics
  mcp-workflow        MCP tool schemas, limits, execution
  safety-recovery     Safe retries and sensitive commands
  debug-checklist    Error classification and next steps


DEEP-DIVE TROUBLESHOOTING
  trace-events, trace-investigation, trace-select
  memory-investigation, memory-symbols, memory-failures
  devices-discovery, devices-gpio, devices-pwm, devices-i2c
  devices-adc, devices-framebuffer, agent-diagnostic-cases
get_manual() lists pages; get_manual(topic) retrieves one page.
Use 'help COMMAND' for CLI usage. Manuals live in XIP ROM and
may exceed RAM file limits. Read one page at a time.
